  "content": {
    "id": "p148-q16-response",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Find the value(s) of $a$ such that $x+y=a$ is tangential to the circle $x^2+y^2=\\frac{9}{2}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=\\pm3$.\n\nSubstitute $y=a-x$ into the circle and set the discriminant to zero.",
      "worked": "Substitute $y=a-x$ into the circle.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+(a-x)^2&=\\frac{9}{2}\\\\2x^2-2ax+a^2-\\frac{9}{2}&=0\\end{align*}$$\nA tangent meets the circle once, so $\\Delta=0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(-2a)^2-4(2)\\left(a^2-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)&=0\\\\36-4a^2&=0\\\\a^2&=9\\\\a&=\\pm3\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 70
  },
